  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1215 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nA very stable/dry weather pattern will linger through the end of the \nweek under a partly cloudy sky with temperatures above normal in the \nupper 70s and lows in the 50s.\n\nA more unsettled pattern will arrive for the weekend mainly for \nSaturday night into the day Sunday as rain chances (20-40%) increase \nacross the Quad State area. This will be the result of increasing \nmoisture and an ill-defined/weakening low pressure system passing \nthrough. Overall totals are expected to be very light, generally \nless than a tenth of an inch for most locations. In fact, there is \nonly around a 30% chance of seeing a quarter inch of rain across the \narea. The later arrival also look little better for Halloween \nfestivities, even thou some nuisance rain may be moving in.\n\nAfter the weekend precipitation, yet another/stronger upper level-\nlow and surface low is expected to approach/move through the Quad \nState through much of next week. The low may end up becoming \ncentered directly overhead or at a minimum upper-level troughing may \nbecome established along with increased moisture. That just means \ntemperatures will trend near normal or cooler than normal with \nchances for showers and possibly a few thunderstorms through at \nleast Wednesday. The latest ensemble guidance continues to hint at a \nbetter chance of seeing substantial rainfall with around a 50% \nchance of seeing 1 inch or more of rain Monday through Wednesday \nevening. There's still some uncertainty on how the systems evolve \nnext week so stay tuned for updates on rainfall totals!",
